theory.answer:
Field names never appear in the encoded bytes — only numeric tags and values do. Validation happens against the `.proto` schema at compile time (generated stubs) and at decode time (parser checks tag + wire type pairs).

Schema in IDL assigns each field a unique tag and type:
```
message Person {
    string user_name = 1;
    int64 favorite_number = 2;
    repeated string interests = 3;
}
```

On the wire: "the encoded data contains field tags, which are numbers (1, 2, and 3). Those are the numbers that appear in the schema definition. Field tags are like aliases for fields." Each field is a `(tag, type annotation, value)` triple. Type and tag are often packed into one byte; integers use variable-length encoding. Lists are repeated occurrences of the same tag — protobuf has no separate array type, only `repeated` in the schema.

Decoding: the parser reads tag numbers, looks up expected types from the compiled schema, and skips unknown tags using the embedded wire-type bits — that skip path is what preserves forward compatibility. The IDL itself does not support rich value constraints (unlike JSON Schema regex/min/max); validation is structural (required types, known tags), not business-rule level.

Renaming `user_name` → `username` in the `.proto` does not change bytes on disk; changing tag `1` to another number breaks every historical record.

Comment: `protoc --decode=Person` dumps tag numbers, not names — the fastest sanity check that names are schema-side only. Buf Schema Registry validates that new `.proto` files don't reuse retired tags.

practice.answer:
Complete `todo_api.py` with v2 `priority` applied. Adding an optional JSON field with a default is the usual REST evolution path: old clients omit it; new servers fill `normal`; old clients ignore unknown response keys.

```python
#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""todo_api.py — complete v2 reference (optional priority)."""
from datetime import datetime, timezone
from enum import Enum
from typing import Optional
from uuid import uuid4

from fastapi import FastAPI, HTTPException, Query
from pydantic import BaseModel

app = FastAPI(title="Todo API", version="2.0.0")


class Priority(str, Enum):
    low = "low"
    normal = "normal"
    high = "high"


class Todo(BaseModel):
    id: str
    title: str
    completed: bool = False
    created_at: str
    priority: Priority = Priority.normal


class TodoCreate(BaseModel):
    title: str
    priority: Priority = Priority.normal


class TodoReplace(BaseModel):
    title: str
    completed: bool
    priority: Priority = Priority.normal


class TodoPatch(BaseModel):
    title: Optional[str] = None
    completed: Optional[bool] = None
    priority: Optional[Priority] = None


STORE: dict[str, dict] = {}


def _now() -> str:
    return datetime.now(timezone.utc).replace(microsecond=0).isoformat()


def _public(row: dict) -> dict:
    return {
        "id": row["id"],
        "title": row["title"],
        "completed": row["completed"],
        "created_at": row["created_at"],
        "priority": row.get("priority", Priority.normal.value),
    }


@app.get("/todos")
def list_todos(limit: int = Query(20, ge=1, le=100), cursor: Optional[str] = None):
    items = sorted(STORE.values(), key=lambda t: t["id"])
    if cursor:
        items = [t for t in items if t["id"] > cursor]
    page = [_public(t) for t in items[:limit]]
    next_cursor = items[limit - 1]["id"] if len(items) > limit else None
    return {"items": page, "next_cursor": next_cursor}


@app.post("/todos", status_code=201)
def create_todo(body: TodoCreate):
    tid = str(uuid4())
    row = {
        "id": tid,
        "title": body.title,
        "completed": False,
        "created_at": _now(),
        "priority": body.priority.value,
    }
    STORE[tid] = row
    return _public(row)


@app.get("/todos/{todo_id}")
def get_todo(todo_id: str):
    row = STORE.get(todo_id)
    if not row:
        raise HTTPException(404, "todo not found")
    return _public(row)


@app.put("/todos/{todo_id}")
def replace_todo(todo_id: str, body: TodoReplace):
    if todo_id not in STORE:
        raise HTTPException(404, "todo not found")
    row = {
        "id": todo_id,
        "title": body.title,
        "completed": body.completed,
        "created_at": STORE[todo_id]["created_at"],
        "priority": body.priority.value,
    }
    STORE[todo_id] = row
    return _public(row)


@app.patch("/todos/{todo_id}")
def patch_todo(todo_id: str, body: TodoPatch):
    row = STORE.get(todo_id)
    if not row:
        raise HTTPException(404, "todo not found")
    data = body.model_dump(exclude_unset=True)
    if "title" in data:
        row["title"] = data["title"]
    if "completed" in data:
        row["completed"] = data["completed"]
    if "priority" in data and data["priority"] is not None:
        row["priority"] = data["priority"].value
    return _public(row)


@app.delete("/todos/{todo_id}", status_code=204)
def delete_todo(todo_id: str):
    if todo_id not in STORE:
        raise HTTPException(404, "todo not found")
    del STORE[todo_id]
    return None
```

```bash
uvicorn todo_api:app --reload --port 8000

# v1-shaped create (no priority) — must default to normal
curl -s -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8000/todos \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{"title":"buy milk"}'

# v2 create
curl -s -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8000/todos \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{"title":"pay rent","priority":"high"}'

# OpenAPI must list priority
curl -s http://127.0.0.1:8000/openapi.json | python3 -c "import sys,json; s=json.load(sys.stdin); print('priority' in str(s['components']['schemas']))"
```

Rolling-deploy notes: optional additive `priority` with default `normal` is safe — old client → new server request (omit priority) is backward compatible (newer code reads older data); new server → old client response (extra priority) is forward compatible (older code reads newer data / ignores unknown keys). Same URL paths and methods stay stable. Breaking changes would include making `priority` required, renaming/removing fields, changing types, or introducing a mandatory new path that old clients cannot call. Prefer keeping `/todos` and documenting additive schema changes in OpenAPI rather than forcing a hard cutover.

v2 (add optional `currency` with default `"USD"`) is both backward compatible (new readers fill missing `currency` from the default) and forward compatible (old readers ignore the unknown field). The gate `ch5_check_forward_safe` only encodes the fields-not-removed half: every old field name must remain so old consumers can still decode new payloads. That matches Confluent FORWARD (and the forward half of FULL), not Confluent BACKWARD (which allows deletions when new consumers can still read older data). v3 (remove `amount_cents` with no default) fails the gate: old readers still need that field.

```sql
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS ch5_events CASCADE;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS ch5_schemas CASCADE;

CREATE TABLE ch5_schemas (
  schema_id   INT PRIMARY KEY,
  schema_json JSONB NOT NULL,
  compatibility TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT 'FORWARD'
);

CREATE TABLE ch5_events (
  id          BIGSERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
  schema_id   INT NOT NULL REFERENCES ch5_schemas(schema_id),
  payload     JSONB NOT NULL,
  created_at  TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT now()
);

INSERT INTO ch5_schemas (schema_id, schema_json) VALUES
(1, '{"type":"record","name":"Order","fields":[
  {"name":"order_id","type":"string"},
  {"name":"amount_cents","type":"int"}
]}');

INSERT INTO ch5_events (schema_id, payload) VALUES
(1, '{"order_id":"ord-100","amount_cents":4999}');

-- Forward-safe / fields-not-removed gate (old consumers reading new schemas)
CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION ch5_check_forward_safe(old_id INT, new_json JSONB)
RETURNS BOOLEAN LANGUAGE plpgsql AS $$
DECLARE
  old_fields JSONB;
  new_fields JSONB;
  f JSONB;
BEGIN
  SELECT schema_json->'fields' INTO old_fields FROM ch5_schemas WHERE schema_id = old_id;
  new_fields := new_json->'fields';
  -- Every old field must exist in new with same name
  FOR f IN SELECT * FROM jsonb_array_elements(old_fields)
  LOOP
    IF NOT EXISTS (
      SELECT 1 FROM jsonb_array_elements(new_fields) nf
      WHERE nf->>'name' = f->>'name'
    ) THEN
      RETURN FALSE;  -- removed field breaks old consumers
    END IF;
  END LOOP;
  RETURN TRUE;
END;
$$;

-- v2: ADD currency with default — passes forward-safe check
SELECT ch5_check_forward_safe(1, '{
  "type":"record","name":"Order","fields":[
    {"name":"order_id","type":"string"},
    {"name":"amount_cents","type":"int"},
    {"name":"currency","type":"string","default":"USD"}
  ]}'::jsonb);
-- → TRUE

INSERT INTO ch5_schemas (schema_id, schema_json) VALUES
(2, '{"type":"record","name":"Order","fields":[
  {"name":"order_id","type":"string"},
  {"name":"amount_cents","type":"int"},
  {"name":"currency","type":"string","default":"USD"}
]}');

INSERT INTO ch5_events (schema_id, payload) VALUES
(2, '{"order_id":"ord-200","amount_cents":1299,"currency":"EUR"}');

-- v3 breaking for old consumers: remove amount_cents — refuse registration
SELECT ch5_check_forward_safe(2, '{
  "type":"record","name":"Order","fields":[
    {"name":"order_id","type":"string"},
    {"name":"currency","type":"string","default":"USD"}
  ]}'::jsonb);
-- → FALSE; do not INSERT schema_id 3

-- Old consumer read path (v1-safe shape + currency default)
SELECT e.schema_id,
       e.payload->>'order_id' AS order_id,
       (e.payload->>'amount_cents')::int AS amount_cents,
       COALESCE(e.payload->>'currency', 'USD') AS currency
FROM   ch5_events e;
```

Deploy order (producer/consumer + registry):
1. Register v2 in the registry after `ch5_check_forward_safe` returns TRUE.
2. Roll out producers that write schema_id=2 (payloads may include `currency`).
3. Old consumers keep projecting order_id + amount_cents and ignore `currency` until they upgrade; new consumers may read `currency` (or COALESCE to USD on older events).

Never reuse field names for new semantics; never remove fields until all readers that need them are upgraded (or you accept a coordinated migration).

Comment: Confluent Schema Registry BACKWARD allows deleting fields (new consumers must read data written with older schemas); FORWARD forbids deleting fields that older consumers still expect. This lab gate mirrors FORWARD / fields-not-removed — check with `POST /compatibility/subjects/.../versions/latest` under FORWARD mode, not BACKWARD.

A is Protocol Buffers (tag/wire-type bytes, no field names). B is minified JSON (UTF-8 starting with `{`, field names visible, `json.loads` succeeds). C is MessagePack (leading `0x83` fixmap of 3 keys, length-prefixed field names on the wire). D is Avro (concatenated values only — decoder needs the writer's schema).

```python
# ch5_identify_encoding.py
# Lab: identify JSON / MessagePack / Protobuf / Avro from hex dumps (DDIA ch5)
from __future__ import annotations

import json
from typing import Literal

Encoding = Literal["json", "messagepack", "protobuf", "avro"]

HEX = {
    "A": "0a044c616d7010031a037265641a04626c7565",  # 19 bytes
    "B": "7b226974656d4e616d65223a224c616d70222c227175616e74697479223a332c22636f6c6f7273223a5b22726564222c22626c7565225d7d",  # 56 bytes
    "C": "83a86974656d4e616d65a44c616d70a87175616e7469747903a6636f6c6f727392a3726564a4626c7565",  # 42 bytes
    "D": "084c616d7006040672656408626c756500",  # 17 bytes
}


def fingerprint(raw: bytes) -> str:
    """Return a short debug string of leading bytes and any JSON decode result."""
    head = " ".join(f"{b:02x}" for b in raw[:8])
    try:
        text = raw.decode("utf-8")
        obj = json.loads(text)
        return f"head=[{head}] json_ok={obj!r}"
    except (UnicodeDecodeError, json.JSONDecodeError):
        return f"head=[{head}] json_ok=False"


def classify(raw: bytes) -> Encoding:
    # JSON: printable UTF-8 object with field names
    try:
        obj = json.loads(raw.decode("utf-8"))
        if isinstance(obj, dict) and "itemName" in obj:
            return "json"
    except (UnicodeDecodeError, json.JSONDecodeError):
        pass

    # MessagePack: fixmap with 3 entries (0x80 | 3 == 0x83), then length-prefixed keys
    if raw and raw[0] == 0x83:
        return "messagepack"

    # Protobuf: first byte is tag 1, wire type 2 (length-delimited string) => 0x0a
    if raw and raw[0] == 0x0a:
        return "protobuf"

    # Avro: no tags or names — values concatenated in schema order
    return "avro"


def decoder_needs(encoding: Encoding) -> str:
    if encoding == "json":
        return "nothing beyond the bytes (self-describing field names)"
    if encoding == "messagepack":
        return "nothing beyond the bytes (self-describing keys like JSON)"
    if encoding == "protobuf":
        return "compiled .proto schema to map tags to field names/types"
    return "writer's Avro schema (bytes alone have no names or tags)"


if __name__ == "__main__":
    for label, hx in HEX.items():
        raw = bytes.fromhex(hx)
        print(f"{label}: len={len(raw)} {fingerprint(raw)}")
        enc = classify(raw)
        print(f"  encoding={enc} needs={decoder_needs(enc)}")
```

Clues per sequence: A has `0x0a` / `0x10` / repeated `0x1a` tags and the string `Lamp` with no preceding name; B is readable UTF-8 JSON; C embeds `itemName` after MsgPack string headers; D is length-prefixed values only (`Lamp`, int 3, array of two strings) and is ambiguous without a schema.

proto3 (hand-authored service contract) — inventory.proto:

```
syntax = "proto3";
message InventoryItem {
  string item_name = 1;
  int64 quantity = 2;
  repeated string colors = 3;
}
```


Avro v1 — inventory_v1.avsc:

```json
{
  "type": "record", "name": "InventoryItem",
  "fields": [
    {"name": "itemName", "type": "string"},
    {"name": "quantity", "type": ["null", "int"], "default": null},
    {"name": "colors", "type": {"type": "array", "items": "string"}}
  ]
}
```


Avro v2 (add warehouse — must have default for backward compat) — inventory_v2.avsc:

```json
{
  "type": "record", "name": "InventoryItem",
  "fields": [
    {"name": "itemName", "type": "string"},
    {"name": "quantity", "type": ["null", "int"], "default": null},
    {"name": "colors", "type": {"type": "array", "items": "string"}},
    {"name": "warehouse", "type": ["null", "string"], "default": null}
  ]
}
```


Reader v2 decoding v1-written record outputs:
```json
{"itemName": "Lamp", "quantity": 3, "colors": ["red", "blue"], "warehouse": null}
```
"If the code reading the data expects a certain field but the writer's schema does not contain a field of that name, it is filled in with a default value declared in the reader's schema."

Reverse translation JSON → protobuf: assign fresh tag numbers, never infer tags from JSON key order. JSON → Avro: map JSON types to Avro primitives; use union with null + default for optional fields.

Comment: `protoc --encode=InventoryItem` and `avro-tools jsontofrag` are the CLI checks; Confluent Schema Registry stores the Avro v2 JSON and enforces that `warehouse` has a default before registration.
